<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Svarožič</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Svarozhich, Zuarasici</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Svarožič was a West Slavic god whose temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rethra">Rethra</a>, among the Lutici, is described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thietmar-of-merseburg">Thietmar of Merseburg</a> around 1015 under the name Zuarasici. His name means 'son of Svarog', and he is often linked with fire or the sun. The missionary Bruno of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/querfurt">Querfurt</a> also names him in 1008 as a god of the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/svarozic.jpg" alt="Svarožič" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Union of Slavic Rodnover Communities (Союз Славянских Общин Славянской Родной Веры) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kupala_2015_in_Krasotinka,_Kaluga_(5).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> sources write the name as Zuarasiz or Zuarasici, and Old East Slavic texts as Svarozhits or Svarozhich. It is formed from the name of Svarog with the suffix -its or -ich, which most scholars read as a patronymic, as Polish panicz, son of the master, comes from pan. Others take the suffix as a diminutive, young or little Svarog, comparing Serbo-Croatian Djurdjić, and Aleksander Brückner pointed to Lithuanian prayers that use the diminutive dievaite. Svarog itself is usually linked with svar, quarrel, and with Old English andswaru, Old Norse sverja and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> svarati.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Svarozhits is interpreted as a god of fire. Indo-European mythologies have a male fire deity, such as Agni or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atar">Atar</a>, and Agni is born from the friction of two querns, Heaven and Earth, so that fire is the effect of union; Svarog, often interpreted as a god of the sky and as a blacksmith who wields fire, would be the father. A Kuyavian folk song of a smith working in a forge on a stone in an open field, whose fire never goes out, is associated with this motif.</p><p>Some researchers identify him with Dazhbog, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a>, quoting the Slavic translation of the Chronicle of John Malalas, depicts as the son of Svarog. Henryk Łowmiański argued that the translation was made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, where the suffix had been forgotten. There is no consensus: the Sermon by the Holy Father Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-chrysostom">John Chrysostom</a> names both gods, which would make them brothers, with Svarozhits matching Agni and Dazhbog matching Surya.</p><p>He is most likely identical with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radegast">Radegast</a>, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a> names as the chief god of Radogost, where earlier sources placed Svarozhits, and whom Helmold calls the god of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obotrites">Obotrites</a>. It has been suggested that Bruno mentioned Saint Maurice, patron saint of knighthood and armed struggle, in his letter because he saw him as the Christian equivalent of Svarozhits.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Svarozhits” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The federation had no prince; decisions were taken by assemblies of the tribes, and Thietmar of Merseburg describes the temple of Radegast (<b>Svarożic</b>) at Rethra, whose priests consulted oracles.</blockquote><a href="/state/lutician-federation">Lutician Federation</a></li></ul></section>
